**The key structural finding.** A ground-network record does not need the RUL tile-shape rules to
be evaluated. SC4 resolved them when the tile was built and saved the answer: each record carries
its four deck corners with position, texture coordinate and baked lighting colour, plus the
already-chosen texture id. Rendering is therefore a direct read — which is why sloped roads,
diagonals and level crossings came out right without touching `RULSubfile.gd`. Verified layout:

```
u32 size, u32 crc, u32 mem, u16 major(8), u16 minor(4)
u16 zot, u8 ?, u8 appearance      (bit 0 = present in city, bit 6 = burnt)
u32 0xC772BF98                    family marker, constant
tract: u8 minX, minZ, maxX, maxZ, u16 xTractSize, zTractSize
u32 sgpropCount + sgprops[]
u32 GID, u32 TID, u32 IID         all zero on ground networks
u8 ?
f32 position x, y, z              metres, tile centre
4 x vertex { f32 x,y,z,u,v; u8 r,g,b,a }        24 bytes each, odd-aligned
u32 textureId                     FSH family; high byte = network family per RULSubfile.gd
u8  wealthTexture
u32 baseTexture                   FSH family for the sidewalk/ground underneath
u8  orientation
u8 x2 ?
u8  crossingCount-1
crossings[] { u8 networkType, west, north, east, south }
u32 wallCount + walls[] { u32 texture, vertex }
bbox: 6 f32 grouped BY AXIS (minX, maxX, minY, maxY, minZ, maxZ)
u32 constructionStates, u32 alternatePathId, 3 x u32 ?, u64 demolishingCosts
```

`networkType`: 0 Road · 1 Rail · 2 Maxis El Highway · 3 Street · 4 Pipe · 5 Powerline ·
6 Avenue · 7 Subway · 8 Light Rail · 9 Monorail · 10 One-Way Road · 11 Dirt Road.

Textures resolve at TGI `(0x7AB50E44, 0x1ABE787D, textureId + zoom)` for zoom 0..4 — the same
scheme `TransitTiles.gd` already used. All 36 distinct texture ids in Big City Tutorial resolve
with all five zoom variants present.

Foundations and retaining walls store a placement block that no community parser documents. It
was decoded here as `f32 x, y, z, size_x, height, size_z` (metres), at record offset 0x38 for
foundations and 0x48 for walls. Evidence: 100% of the 1,245 walls and 2,510 foundations in Big
City Tutorial then have their centre on a lot-covered tile (the x/z transpose gives ~55%), and
the median of `y − terrain altitude` is exactly 0.00 m. Their exemplars carry no model for most
records — instead a five-entry list of FSH zoom variants plus the world size one repeat covers
(`0x295961F2`/`0x295961F3` for walls, `0x68FCFF37`/`0xC911EDA0` for foundations), i.e. SC4
stretches a texture over a box. A minority carry an RKT1 S3D model instead.

**What is still undecoded.** The network index's per-entry payload: between consecutive
`{tile_number, mem, subfile_type}` triples sits a variable-length block nobody has decoded, and
SC4Parser's own attempt cannot keep the cursor aligned. `NetworkIndexSubfile.gd` resynchronises
by scanning for the next triple instead, which recovers exactly the declared reference count in
every populated save. What `tile_number` means is also open — it is not a row-major tile index
(both that and its transpose match only about half the entries against the actual tile
coordinates of the ground-network records).

